You are given a code snippet that represents a method `mean_recv_time` within a packet management system. The method takes a `time_avg` parameter of type `Duration` and returns an `Option<Duration>`. The `recv_packets` field is a collection of received packets, each containing information about the time it was sent and the time it was received.

The `mean_recv_time` method should calculate the average time that packets take to return over a period of time specified by `time_avg`. It filters the received packets based on the time difference between their sent and received times and then calculates the average time for the filtered packets.

Your task is to complete the implementation of the `mean_recv_time` method to correctly calculate the average time for the received packets that meet the specified time criteria.

Complete the implementation of the `mean_recv_time` method to calculate the average time for the received packets that meet the specified time criteria. Ensure that the method handles the case when there are no received packets by returning `None`.

```rust
use std::time::Duration;

struct Packet {
    sent: Duration,
    received: Option<Duration>,
}

struct PacketManager {
    recv_packets: Vec<Packet>,
}

impl PacketManager {
    /// Calculate the average time that packets are taking to return over a period of time.
    pub fn mean_recv_time(&self, time_avg: Duration) -> Option<Duration> {
        if self.recv_packets.is_empty() {
            return None;
        }
        let avg: Vec<_> = self
            .recv_packets
            .iter()
            .filter(|x| (x.sent + x.received.unwrap()).elapsed() < time_avg)
            .collect();
        let avg_len = avg.len().max(1);
        let tot_time: Duration = avg.iter().fold(Duration::from_micros(0), |acc, x| {
            // Calculate the total time for the filtered packets
            acc + x.received.unwrap() - x.sent
        });
        Some(tot_time / avg_len as u32)
    }
}
```

