fn fetch_operand(&self, bus: &mut Bus, mode: AddressingMode) -> Operand {
    let addr = self.reg.PC + 1;
    match mode {
        AddressingMode::Immediate => {
            let operand_value = bus.load(addr);
            Operand::Immediate(operand_value)
        }
        AddressingMode::Absolute => {
            let address = u16::from_le_bytes([bus.load(addr), bus.load(addr + 1)]);
            let operand_value = bus.load(address);
            Operand::Absolute(operand_value)
        }
        AddressingMode::Register => {
            let register_index = bus.load(addr) & 0x0F; // Lower 4 bits
            let operand_value = self.registers[register_index as usize];
            Operand::Register(operand_value)
        }
    }
}
```

In the solution, the fetch_operand method is completed using a match statement to handle the different addressing modes. For Immediate mode, the operand is fetched directly from the memory location pointed to by PC+1. For Absolute mode, the 16-bit address is retrieved from the next two memory locations, and the operand is fetched from the specified address. For Register mode, the lower 4 bits of the byte at PC+1 are used to select the CPU register, and the operand is fetched from the selected register. The appropriate Operand variant is returned based on the addressing mode.

