Your task is to complete the `countWordOccurrences` function to read the content of the file specified by `filename` and count the occurrences of the word specified by `word`. The function should return the total count of the word in the file, ignoring case sensitivity and considering only whole word matches.

Assume that the input file specified by `filename` exists and is accessible for reading. The word to be counted will not contain any punctuation marks or special characters, and will only consist of alphabetic characters.

Example:
Given the file "sample.txt" with the following content:
```
The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog. The dog is not as quick as the fox.
```
If the word to be counted is "the", the function should return 3, as "the" appears three times as whole words in the file.

response:
```cpp
#include <fstream>
#include <sstream>
#include <algorithm>

int countWordOccurrences(const std::string& filename, const std::string& word) {
    std::ifstream file(filename);
    if (!file.is_open()) {
        // Handle file open error
        return -1;
    }

    std::string content((std::istreambuf_iterator<char>(file)), std::istreambuf_iterator<char>());
    file.close();

    std::transform(content.begin(), content.end(), content.begin(), ::tolower);
    std::transform(word.begin(), word.end(), word.begin(), ::tolower);

    std::istringstream iss(content);
    std::string token;
    int count = 0;
    while (iss >> token) {
        if (token == word) {
            count++;
        }
    }

    return count;
}
```

This solution first opens the file specified by `filename` and reads its content into a string. It then converts both the file content and the word to be counted to lowercase to ensure case insensitivity. After that, it tokenizes the content into words and counts the occurrences of the specified word, returning the total count.

